# EphCore.Stars — Fixed Stars Tour

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:eph_core, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
    {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
    {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
  ],
  config: [
    eph_core: [
      # Planetary DE440s only. Avoid config_path: :eph_core here — that loads
      # config.exs asteroid kernels before SPK.Server starts.
      kernel_base_dir: Path.expand("../../priv", __DIR__),
      spk_kernels: ["de440s.bsp"]
    ]
  ]
)

Logger.configure(level: :warning)
```

## Overview

The planets move; the "fixed" stars only *seem* to. `EphCore.Stars` loads the
**Hipparcos** catalog, applies each star's **proper motion** from the J1991.25
catalog epoch, and projects the result into ecliptic, equatorial-of-date, and
local horizon coordinates.

Two tiny modules do the work:

* `EphCore.Stars.Catalog` — parses `priv/stars/hip_main.dat` once, caches it in
  `:persistent_term`, and exposes `lookup/1`, `all_significant/0`, and a curated
  HIP → traditional-name map (`significance_list/0`).
* `EphCore.Stars.Position` — `compute/3` takes a star record + `jd_tt` and
  returns RA/Dec (of date), ecliptic lon/lat, and optional alt/az.

**How to open:** Install [Livebook](https://livebook.dev/), open this `.livemd`,
evaluate the setup cell. The Hipparcos catalog is **optional** — fixed-star cells
need `priv/stars/hip_main.dat`. From the repo root:

```bash
mix eph.setup_stars
```

That downloads ~51 MB into `priv/stars/`. Planetary ephemeris works without it;
see `README.md` for manual download if the task fails.

## Helpers

```elixir
alias VegaLite, as: Vl
alias EphCore.Stars.{Catalog, Position}
alias EphCore.Corrections.ApparentPlace
alias EphCore.SnapshotPipeline

# Kino.Input.utc_datetime/2 reads as NaiveDateTime (UTC) or DateTime depending on version.
read_utc = fn input ->
  case Kino.Input.read(input) do
    %DateTime{} = dt -> DateTime.truncate(dt, :second)
    %NaiveDateTime{} = ndt -> ndt |> DateTime.from_naive!("Etc/UTC") |> DateTime.truncate(:second)
  end
end

:ok
```

## Ready check

```elixir
priv = EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.base_dir()
catalog_file = Path.join([priv, "stars", "hip_main.dat"])
stars = Catalog.all_significant()

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**EphCore** `#{Application.spec(:eph_core, :vsn)}` · priv `#{priv}`

Hipparcos catalog: #{if File.exists?(catalog_file), do: "✅ present", else: "❌ missing — run `mix eph.setup_stars` from repo root"}
· **#{length(stars)}** named stars loaded.
""")
```

## 1 — The named-star catalog

`Catalog.all_significant/0` returns the curated bright stars with their raw
J1991.25 catalog astrometry: position (RA/Dec), proper motion (mas/yr),
parallax, and visual magnitude.

```elixir
catalog_rows =
  for s <- Enum.sort_by(Catalog.all_significant(), & &1.magnitude) do
    %{
      hip: s.hip,
      name: s.name,
      mag: s.magnitude,
      ra_deg: Float.round(s.ra_deg, 4),
      dec_deg: Float.round(s.dec_deg, 4),
      pm_ra_mas_yr: s.pm_ra_mas_yr,
      pm_dec_mas_yr: s.pm_dec_mas_yr,
      parallax_mas: s.parallax_mas
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(catalog_rows, name: "Hipparcos named stars (J1991.25 epoch)")
```

Total proper motion `μ = √(μ_α*² + μ_δ²)` tells you how fast each star slides
across the sky. Barnard's-star-fast movers stand out immediately.

```elixir
pm_rows =
  for s <- Catalog.all_significant() do
    mu = :math.sqrt(s.pm_ra_mas_yr * s.pm_ra_mas_yr + s.pm_dec_mas_yr * s.pm_dec_mas_yr)
    %{name: s.name, total_pm_mas_yr: Float.round(mu, 1)}
  end

Vl.new(width: 520, height: 260, title: "Total proper motion (mas/yr)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(pm_rows)
|> Vl.mark(:bar)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "name", type: :nominal, sort: "-x", title: nil)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "total_pm_mas_yr", type: :quantitative, title: "μ (mas/yr)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "total_pm_mas_yr", type: :quantitative, scale: [scheme: "plasma"])
```

## 2 — A star's coordinates drift over the millennia

`Position.compute/2` (no observer) applies proper motion *and* returns RA/Dec in
the **equator of date**, so the reported position walks for two reasons:

* **Precession of the equinox** — the ~50″/yr swing of the coordinate grid
  itself (the dominant term over long spans: ~111° of RA over 8000 years).
* **Proper motion** — the star's own space motion, layered on top. **Arcturus**
  is a famous fast mover (~2.3″/yr), so its residual drift is unusually large.

Let's track Arcturus from 3000 BC to 5000 AD.

```elixir
{:ok, arcturus} = Catalog.lookup(69673)

# DateTime can't hold BCE years, so step jd_tt directly off the J2000 epoch.
j2000_tt = ApparentPlace.utc_to_jd_tt(~U[2000-01-01 12:00:00Z])

pm_track =
  for year <- -3000..5000//500 do
    jd_tt = j2000_tt + (year - 2000) * 365.25
    pos = Position.compute(arcturus, jd_tt)

    %{
      year: year,
      ra_deg: Float.round(pos.ra_deg, 4),
      dec_deg: Float.round(pos.dec_deg, 4)
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(pm_track, name: "Arcturus RA/Dec drift (proper motion only)")
```

```elixir
Vl.new(width: 520, height: 320, title: "Arcturus proper-motion track (RA vs Dec)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(pm_track)
|> Vl.mark(:line, point: true)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "ra_deg", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false], title: "RA of date (°)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "dec_deg", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false], title: "Dec of date (°)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:order, "year", type: :quantitative)
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "year", type: :quantitative, scale: [scheme: "turbo"])
```

The long sweep you see is mostly the equinox precessing under the star. To
isolate Arcturus's *own* motion, look at its raw catalog proper motion from §1
(~2.3″/yr): ~5° of true space motion accumulates over these 8000 years — small
next to precession, but "fixed" is still only a matter of timescale.

## 3 — Tonight's bright-star board

Pick an observer and instant. Local sidereal time comes from the validated
snapshot pipeline (request `:gast` for apparent sidereal time), then feeds
`Position.compute/3` to project each star to altitude / azimuth.

```elixir
lat_in = Kino.Input.number("Latitude (°)", default: 44.9778)
lon_in = Kino.Input.number("Longitude (°)", default: -93.2650)
height_in = Kino.Input.number("Height (m)", default: 250)
utc_in = Kino.Input.utc_datetime("UTC", default: ~N[2026-07-21 04:00:00])

Kino.Layout.grid([lat_in, lon_in, height_in, utc_in], columns: 2)
```

```elixir
observer = %{
  lat_deg: Kino.Input.read(lat_in),
  lon_deg: Kino.Input.read(lon_in),
  height_m: Kino.Input.read(height_in)
}

utc = read_utc.(utc_in)
jd_tt = ApparentPlace.utc_to_jd_tt(utc)

# LAST (local apparent sidereal time) from the pipeline's earth-orientation pass.
loc = %{lat: observer.lat_deg, lon: observer.lon_deg, height: observer.height_m}
{:ok, obs_sun} = SnapshotPipeline.observe(utc, loc, [:sun], models: %{earth_orientation: :gast})
last_deg = obs_sun.observer_position.last_deg

Kino.Markdown.new("""
Observer `#{observer.lat_deg}, #{observer.lon_deg}` · **#{DateTime.to_iso8601(utc)}**
· LAST = **#{Float.round(last_deg, 3)}°** · JD(TT) = #{Float.round(jd_tt, 5)}
""")
```

```elixir
sky_rows =
  for s <- Catalog.all_significant() do
    pos = Position.compute(s, jd_tt, observer: observer, lst_deg: last_deg)

    %{
      name: pos.name,
      mag: pos.magnitude,
      altitude_deg: Float.round(pos.altitude_deg, 2),
      azimuth_deg: Float.round(pos.azimuth_deg, 2),
      ra_deg: Float.round(pos.ra_deg, 3),
      dec_deg: Float.round(pos.dec_deg, 3),
      up?: pos.altitude_deg > 0
    }
  end
  |> Enum.sort_by(& &1.altitude_deg, :desc)

Kino.DataTable.new(sky_rows, name: "Named stars — local alt/az")
```

```elixir
Vl.new(width: 560, height: 320, title: "Star altitude (above/below horizon)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(sky_rows)
|> Vl.mark(:bar)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "name", type: :nominal, sort: "-y", title: nil)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "altitude_deg", type: :quantitative, title: "Altitude (°)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "up?", type: :nominal, title: "Above horizon?")
```

A sky-dome view: azimuth around, altitude as distance from the zenith. Points
near the top of the plot are high overhead; points below the horizon line are
not currently visible.

```elixir
Vl.new(width: 460, height: 360, title: "Sky dome — azimuth vs altitude")
|> Vl.data_from_values(Enum.filter(sky_rows, & &1.up?))
|> Vl.mark(:point, filled: true, size: 140)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "azimuth_deg",
  type: :quantitative,
  scale: [domain: [0, 360]],
  title: "Azimuth (° from N through E)"
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "altitude_deg",
  type: :quantitative,
  scale: [domain: [0, 90]],
  title: "Altitude (°)"
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "mag", type: :quantitative, scale: [scheme: "viridis", reverse: true])
|> Vl.encode_field(:size, "mag", type: :quantitative, scale: [reverse: true])
```

## 4 — Single-star deep dive

Look up any HIP number and see every coordinate the module produces. The full
star field of `hip_main.dat` is loaded — not just the named list — so obscure
HIP numbers work too.

```elixir
hip_in = Kino.Input.text("Hipparcos number", default: "91262")
Kino.render(hip_in)

hip = hip_in |> Kino.Input.read() |> String.trim() |> String.to_integer()

case Catalog.lookup(hip) do
  {:ok, star} ->
    pos = Position.compute(star, jd_tt, observer: observer, lst_deg: last_deg)

    Kino.Markdown.new("""
    ## HIP #{star.hip}#{if star.name, do: " — #{star.name}", else: ""}

    | Quantity | Value |
    |----------|-------|
    | Visual magnitude | #{star.magnitude} |
    | RA (of date) | #{Float.round(pos.ra_deg, 5)}° |
    | Dec (of date) | #{Float.round(pos.dec_deg, 5)}° |
    | Ecliptic longitude | #{Float.round(pos.ecliptic_lon_deg, 5)}° |
    | Ecliptic latitude | #{Float.round(pos.ecliptic_lat_deg, 5)}° |
    | Altitude | #{Float.round(pos.altitude_deg, 3)}° |
    | Azimuth | #{Float.round(pos.azimuth_deg, 3)}° |
    | Proper motion (RA) | #{star.pm_ra_mas_yr} mas/yr |
    | Proper motion (Dec) | #{star.pm_dec_mas_yr} mas/yr |
    | Parallax | #{star.parallax_mas} mas (≈ #{Float.round(1000.0 / max(star.parallax_mas, 1.0e-6), 2)} pc) |
    """)

  {:error, :not_found} ->
    Kino.Markdown.new("HIP #{hip} not found in the catalog.")
end
```

## Pitfalls & notes

1. **`compute/3` needs `lst_deg` for alt/az** — pass both `:observer` and
   `:lst_deg`; omit them and `altitude_deg`/`azimuth_deg` come back `nil`
   (ecliptic + equatorial coordinates still populate).

2. **RA/Dec are equator-*of-date*** — the module precesses the ICRF vector with
   the IAU 2006 matrix, so values won't match a J2000 catalog printout by the
   accumulated precession since 2000.

3. **No aberration or parallax-of-motion here** — `Stars.Position` applies
   proper motion + precession only. For planetary-grade apparent places see
   `../01_observe_pipeline/05_apparent_place.livemd`; annual aberration shifts stars by ~20″ too.

4. **Catalog loads lazily into `:persistent_term`** — the first `lookup/1` or
   `all_significant/0` parses the whole file; subsequent reads are lock-free.

## Key ideas

* **Nothing is fixed** — proper motion is real astrometry, not a rounding error.
  Arcturus and Barnard's Star visibly relocate within recorded history.
* **`:persistent_term` as a star atlas** — the whole Hipparcos catalog lives in a
  read-optimized term, parsed once at first touch. No DB, no GenServer.
* **Same horizon math as the planets** — stars reuse
  `Geometry.Horizontal.alt_az_from_direction/2`, the exact function the snapshot
  pipeline uses for the Sun and Moon. One geometry layer, many bodies.
